#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184051 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a54dcd1d-56f5-527d-9f24-ff4b7d221d06" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position Vector of a Particle Moving With Constant Velocity"}] :question/id #uuid "8f36c3e6-0c27-5b89-9f8d-5ec6b2f9e614" :question/math-academy-id "e-9821" :question/problem "A particle starts moving from the point with position vector $(3\\mathbf{j} - \\mathbf{i}) \\: \\textrm{mi}$ with a constant velocity of $(\\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j}) \\: \\textrm{mi/h}$. Find the position vector of the particle after $2$ hours." :question/worked-solution "If a particle moves with constant velocity $\\mathbf{u}$, the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{r}_0 + \\mathbf{u} t\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}_0$ is the initial position of the particle.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}_0 = 3\\mathbf{j} - \\mathbf{i}, \\qquad \\mathbf u = \\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after\n\n$$\nt = 2 \\: \\textrm{h}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= r_{0} + ut \\\\\n&= (3j - i) + (i - j)(2) \\\\\n&= (-i + 3j) + (2i - 2j) \\\\\n&= (i + j)mi\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184052 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "da1d8490-fd41-5363-97d2-91b360f10723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position Vector of a Particle Moving With Constant Acceleration"}] :question/id #uuid "2d8b9078-4e19-5f6d-b4cb-95f0c7ee07d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9822" :question/problem "A particle starts moving from the point with position vector $(4\\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j})\\,\\textrm{cm}$ with an acceleration of $(\\mathbf{i} + 3\\mathbf{j}) \\, \\textrm{cm/min}^2$ and an initial velocity of $(\\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j}) \\: \\textrm{cm/min}$. Find the position vector of the particle after $4$ minutes." :question/worked-solution "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$, the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{r}_0 + \\mathbf{u} t + \\dfrac12 \\mathbf{a} t^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}_0$ is the initial position of the particle and $\\mathbf{u}$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}_0 = 4\\mathbf i - \\mathbf j, \\qquad \\mathbf u = \\mathbf{i} +\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad \\mathbf a = \\mathbf{i} + 3\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after\n\n$$\nt = 4 \\, \\textrm{min}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= (4i - j) + (i + j)(4) + \\frac{1}{2}(i + 3j)(4)^{2} \\\\\n&= (4i - j) + (4i + 4j) + 8(i + 3j) \\\\\n&= (8i + 3j) + (8i + 24j) \\\\\n&= (16i + 27j)cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184053 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3048320d-7346-53fd-819b-d6e32e4fc02c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between a Particle and the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "ca75ba70-5bef-5c21-ba01-1ad50faa0da2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9824" :question/problem "A particle moves from the origin with an acceleration of $(\\mathbf{i} +\\mathbf{j}) \\, \\textrm{cm/s}^2$ and an initial velocity of $(-\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}) \\: \\textrm{cm/s}$. Find the distance from the particle to the origin after $6$ seconds." :question/worked-solution "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $\\mathbf{a}$, the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{r}_0 + \\mathbf{u} t + \\dfrac12 \\mathbf{a} t^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}_0$ is the initial position of the particle and $\\mathbf{u}$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}_0 = 0\\mathbf i +0 \\mathbf j, \\qquad \\mathbf u = -\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad \\mathbf a = \\mathbf{i} +\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after\n\n$$\nt = 6 \\, \\textrm{s}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= (0i + 0j) + (-i - 2j)(6) + \\frac{1}{2}(i + j)(6)^{2} \\\\\n&= (-6i - 12j) + 18(i + j) \\\\\n&= (-6i - 12j) + (18i + 18j) \\\\\n&= (12i + 6j)cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance from the particle to the origin at this time is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}r & &= & 12i + 6j\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{12^{2} + 6^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{144 + 36} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{180} \\\\\n&= 6\\sqrt{5}cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "da1d8490-fd41-5363-97d2-91b360f10723" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Position Vector of a Particle Moving With Constant Acceleration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82515029-ef80-5856-8473-0e3594c819ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4i - 7j)mm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180916} {:answer/id #uuid "a1af649c-4c57-558c-946d-9c1b1c46f4ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3i - 8j)mm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180917} {:answer/id #uuid "b69a14f2-99c6-5559-8135-e02bf03ad4d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9i - 64j)mm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180918} {:answer/id #uuid "5d394f2c-963a-57ac-a500-87e961faaff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(16i + 49j)mm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180919} {:answer/id #uuid "6bcf8e0d-9ef8-5524-968d-8aede164ebe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(25i + 36j)mm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b69a14f2-99c6-5559-8135-e02bf03ad4d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9i - 64j)mm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180918} :answer-field/id #uuid "a75baba6-665e-5c89-9a77-16bdf62617ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186180915}] :question/id #uuid "7b69163c-7d60-5882-a4d4-0cf0337f664b" :question/math-academy-id "q-136413" :question/problem "A particle starts moving from the point with position vector $(-i + j)mm$ with an acceleration of $(2i - 6j)mm/min^{2}$ and an initial velocity of $(-3i + 2j)mm/min$. Find the position vector of the particle after $5$ minutes."}]}
